SUS="Energy from sources that are naturally replenished and will not run out::replenished,renewed,not run out,never run out,naturally|Use does not cause lasting harm to the environment::harm,environment,pollution,clean|Important for Ghana's future because fossil fuels run out and cause climate change::future,fossil,run out,climate"
REN="Renewable sources are continuously replenished by natural processes::replenished,continuously,naturally|Examples: sunlight, wind, flowing water, biogas::solar,sunlight,wind,water,hydro,biogas|Non-renewable sources formed over millions of years and will run out::millions of years,run out,finite|Examples: coal, crude oil, natural gas, wood or charcoal::coal,oil,gas,charcoal,wood"
SOLAR="Advantage: sunlight all year, good for rural electrification::sunlight,rural,all year|Advantage: low running cost or no pollution::low running,no pollution,pollution,running cost|Disadvantage: high initial cost of panels::initial cost,expensive,cost|Disadvantage: only works when sunlight is available, needs batteries at night::sunlight,night,battery,batteries,storage"
BIOG="Anaerobic bacteria decompose organic waste such as animal dung and food waste::bacteria,anaerobic,decompose,dung,waste|They produce methane gas used for cooking or electricity::methane,gas,cooking|Useful because it uses farm waste and reduces reliance on firewood and deforestation::firewood,deforestation,farm waste,farm"
GEF="Sunlight reaches the Earth as short-wave radiation and the surface absorbs it::sunlight,short,absorbs,radiation|The Earth releases it as long-wave infrared (heat)::infrared,heat,long-wave|Greenhouse gases trap some of this heat so it cannot escape::trap,traps,gases,greenhouse|This warms the Earth::warm,warming"
GHG="Carbon dioxide: burning fossil fuels or deforestation::carbon dioxide,co2,fossil,deforestation|Methane: livestock, rice paddies, landfill sites or gas leaks::methane,livestock,cattle,rice,landfill|Nitrous oxide: fertilisers::nitrous,fertiliser,fertilisers|Water vapour: evaporation from warmer oceans::water vapour,evaporation"
CCW="Rising temperatures reduce crop yields::temperatures,heat,yields|Changes in rainfall: drought in the north, floods in the south::rainfall,drought,flood,floods|Sea level rise floods coastal communities::sea,coastal,flooding,erosion|Loss of biodiversity::biodiversity,extinction|Less water in rivers and dams::dams,volta,water levels|Food insecurity::food,maize,yam,cocoa"
STEPS="Identify the problem (survey energy use and waste)::identify,problem,survey|Research solutions::research,solutions|Set clear goals::goals,targets|Plan the project (resources, tasks)::plan,resources,budget|Carry out the project::carry out,implement,install|Monitor and evaluate::monitor,evaluate|Report and share::report,share"
QA=[
S("Define sustainable energy and explain why it is important for Ghana's future",3,"Sustainable energy is energy from sources that are naturally replenished and will not run out, and whose use does not cause lasting harm to the environment. It is important because fossil fuels will run out and burning them causes climate change.",SUS),
S("State the difference between renewable and non-renewable energy sources",6,"Renewable sources are continuously replenished (sunlight, wind, flowing water, biogas). Non-renewable sources formed over millions of years and will run out (coal, crude oil, natural gas).",REN),
S("Explain two advantages and two disadvantages of solar energy",4,"Advantages: Ghana has sunlight all year, which suits rural electrification, and running costs are low with no pollution. Disadvantages: the panels are expensive to install and make electricity only when the sun shines, so batteries are needed.",SOLAR),
S("Describe how biogas is produced and explain why it is particularly useful",3,"Anaerobic bacteria decompose organic waste such as animal dung and food waste in a digester to produce methane gas for cooking and electricity. It uses farm waste and reduces the use of firewood, so it reduces deforestation.",BIOG),
S("Explain why wood and charcoal are considered unsustainable",2,"Ghana cuts trees down faster than they regrow, so at the current rate of use they cannot last. Burning them also releases CO₂ and causes deforestation, soil erosion and indoor air pollution.",
  "Trees are cut faster than they regrow, so the use is not sustainable::faster,regrow,not sustainable,unsustainable,current rate|Burning releases CO₂ and causes deforestation or erosion::co2,carbon dioxide,deforestation,erosion,pollution"),
S("What is a green economy? Give three examples",4,"A green economy creates jobs and wealth while protecting the environment. Examples: solar street lights, organic farming, biogas from farm waste, electric motorcycles, plastic recycling, energy-efficient buildings.",
  "It creates jobs and wealth while protecting the environment::jobs,wealth,protect,protecting,environment|An example such as clean energy, solar street lights or rooftop solar::solar,clean energy,wind,hydro|An example such as organic farming or biogas::organic,agroforestry,biogas,sustainable agriculture|An example such as green buildings, e-bikes, recycling or composting::recycling,compost,composting,e-bikes,electric,buildings,transport"),
S("Explain why hydroelectric power is important for Ghana",3,"Akosombo, Kpong and Bui dams already produce most of Ghana's electricity, and it is clean and reliable when water is adequate. But it depends on rainfall so drought reduces output, and dams displace communities and affect river ecosystems.",
  "The dams (Akosombo, Kpong, Bui) already provide most of Ghana's electricity::akosombo,kpong,bui,dams|It is clean and well established::clean,established,reliable|It depends on rainfall so drought reduces output::rainfall,drought,water levels|Dams displace communities and affect ecosystems::displace,ecosystem,ecosystems,communities"),
S("A community in northern Ghana currently relies on diesel generators",4,"Solar panels or mini-grids, because northern Ghana has sunlight most of the year and is far from the grid; biogas from animal dung, because farms provide waste and it replaces firewood.",
  "Solar energy::solar|Because there is plenty of sunlight (and the community is far from the grid)::sunlight,grid,rural|Biogas (or another alternative such as wind)::biogas,wind|With a reason such as farm waste or good wind potential::waste,dung,farm,potential"),
S("How does switching from firewood to biogas benefit both the farmer and the environment",2,"The farmer uses farm waste, saves money and gets a rich slurry as fertiliser; the environment benefits because fewer trees are cut (less deforestation) and less CO₂ and smoke are released.",
  "Farmer: uses farm waste for cooking, reduces reliance on firewood and fuel cost::waste,cooking,cost,fertiliser,slurry,firewood|Environment: reduces deforestation and CO₂::deforestation,co2,carbon dioxide,trees,smoke"),
S("Describe how the Ghanaian government is trying to develop a green economy",2,"It has included green economy principles in national development plans, and signed the Paris Agreement in 2016 with targets to expand renewable energy, reduce deforestation and promote sustainable agriculture.",
  "Green economy principles are in national development plans::development plans,plans,principles|Signed the Paris Agreement (2016)::paris|Targets to expand renewable energy::renewable|Targets to reduce deforestation or promote sustainable agriculture::deforestation,agriculture"),
S("Explain the difference between photovoltaic (PV) solar panels and solar thermal systems",2,"PV cells convert sunlight directly into electricity, while solar thermal systems use sunlight to heat water.","PV cells convert sunlight directly to electricity::pv,photovoltaic,electricity|Solar thermal systems use sunlight to heat water::thermal,heat,water"),
S("A village of 200 families in the Upper East Region",6,"Use solar panels (mini-grid) for lighting and phones because of strong sunlight; use biogas digesters with dung from the large animal farms for cooking instead of firewood. Benefits: no diesel fumes or CO₂, less deforestation and cheaper energy.",
  "Solar energy for electricity (strong sunlight, no grid)::solar,sunlight,electricity|Biogas from animal dung for cooking::biogas,dung,cooking|Environmental benefit: no pollution or CO₂ from solar::pollution,co2,carbon dioxide,clean|Environmental benefit: less deforestation or waste used from biogas::deforestation,firewood,waste,trees"),
S("Ghana's electricity supply sometimes fails during the dry season",4,"In the dry season there is less rainfall so the water level in the Volta falls and less water turns the turbines, so output drops. Solar (sunny dry season) and biogas or wind could supplement it.",
  "Lower rainfall means lower water levels and less water to turn the turbines::rainfall,water levels,turbines,drought,less water|Solar energy works well in the dry season::solar,sunlight|Wind or biogas as another alternative::wind,biogas|Justification such as renewable, clean or available::renewable,clean,available,potential"),
S("Explain the greenhouse effect in your own words",4,"Sunlight reaches the Earth as short-wave radiation and the surface absorbs it and releases it as long-wave infrared heat. Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and methane trap some of this heat and prevent it escaping, warming the Earth.",GEF),
S("State four greenhouse gases and give one major human source of each",8,"Carbon dioxide (burning fossil fuels), methane (livestock), nitrous oxide (fertilisers), water vapour (evaporation from warmer oceans).",GHG),
S("Explain the difference between global warming and climate change",2,"Global warming is the rise in average temperatures caused by the enhanced greenhouse effect, while climate change is the broader long-term shifts in weather patterns caused by global warming.",
  "Global warming is the rise in average temperatures caused by the enhanced greenhouse effect::temperature,temperatures,rise,increase,warming|Climate change is the broader long-term change in weather patterns::weather,patterns,long-term,broader,shifts"),
S("Describe three specific ways in which climate change is already affecting Ghana",3,"Later and unpredictable rains and drought reduce crop yields; rising sea levels threaten coastal communities with flooding and erosion; lower water levels in Lake Volta affect hydroelectric power; heavier rain floods cities.",CCW),
S("Explain how deforestation in Ghana contributes to the greenhouse effect in two ways",2,"Trees absorb CO₂, so cutting them down removes this absorption; burning the trees releases CO₂ into the atmosphere.","Trees absorb CO₂, so cutting them reduces the absorption::absorb,absorbs,absorption,less|Burning the trees releases CO₂::burning,burn,releases,release"),
S("State three things that individuals and communities in Ghana can do to help reduce greenhouse gas emissions",3,"Switch from firewood and charcoal to biogas or LPG; use LED lighting; plant trees; reduce, reuse and recycle waste.",
  "Switch from firewood or charcoal to biogas or LPG::biogas,lpg,firewood,charcoal|Use energy-efficient LED lighting::led|Plant trees (reforestation)::trees,plant,reforestation|Reduce, reuse and recycle waste::reduce,reuse,recycle,waste"),
S("What is the Paris Agreement",3,"It is the Paris Agreement on Climate Change, signed by Ghana in 2016. Ghana committed to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions and move to cleaner energy, expanding renewable energy, reducing deforestation and promoting sustainable agriculture.",
  "An agreement on climate change signed by Ghana in 2016::climate change,2016,signed|Ghana committed to reduce greenhouse gas emissions::reduce,emissions|And to move to cleaner energy, renewable energy, less deforestation::cleaner,renewable,deforestation,agriculture"),
S("Explain how rising sea levels caused by climate change could affect fishing communities",2,"Low-lying coastal communities and fishing villages face flooding and erosion, so homes could be flooded and destroyed.","Flooding of low-lying coastal communities::flood,flooding,coastal,low-lying|Erosion that destroys homes::erosion,homes,destroy"),
S("Why does climate change tend to affect poor communities and poor countries",2,"Poor communities depend more directly on agriculture and natural resources, have less capacity to adapt, and have fewer resources to rebuild after floods or droughts.",
  "They depend more directly on agriculture and natural resources::agriculture,farming,depend,natural resources|They have less capacity to adapt and fewer resources to rebuild::adapt,rebuild,resources,less"),
S("Explain how planting trees (reforestation) helps reduce the greenhouse effect",2,"Trees absorb CO₂ from the atmosphere through photosynthesis and store it as carbon.","Trees absorb CO₂ from the atmosphere::absorb,absorbs,co2,carbon dioxide|Through photosynthesis, storing it as carbon::photosynthesis,store,carbon"),
S("A student says that Ghana is a small country",3,"I do not agree. Greenhouse gases mix in the global atmosphere, so everyone's emissions together cause warming, and Ghana already suffers its effects. Ghana's actions such as using LEDs, biogas and planting trees also save money and protect forests.",
  "Disagree: greenhouse gases spread through the whole atmosphere and everyone's emissions add up::atmosphere,global,everyone,all countries,add up|Ghana already suffers climate change effects::suffers,effects,affected,affecting|Actions like LEDs, biogas and trees help and also save money::led,biogas,trees,save,money,deforestation"),
S("A farmer in the Brong-Ahafo Region of Ghana has noticed that rains arrive later",5,"The changes are caused by global warming: human activities such as burning fossil fuels and deforestation add extra greenhouse gases, which trap more heat. Evidence includes later and unpredictable rains, droughts and rising sea levels. The farmer could plant trees, use sustainable farming such as organic farming and agroforestry, and use biogas instead of firewood.",
  "The cause is extra greenhouse gases from human activities, not witchcraft::greenhouse,gases,human,activities,fossil,deforestation|Which trap more heat and cause global warming and climate change::trap,heat,warming,climate|Evidence: later or unpredictable rains, droughts, rising seas::rains,drought,rainfall,sea,evidence|Adaptation: plant trees or use sustainable farming::trees,agroforestry,organic,sustainable,farming"),
S("You have been asked to give a short talk at your school assembly",5,"Sunlight warms the Earth and greenhouse gases trap some heat (the greenhouse effect), but extra gases cause global warming. Effects on Ghana include droughts, floods and rising sea levels. Students can switch off lights, use LED bulbs, plant trees and recycle waste.",
  "What the greenhouse effect is::greenhouse,trap,heat,gases|Two effects of climate change on Ghana::drought,flood,floods,sea,rainfall,yields,temperatures|Three actions: LED bulbs, plant trees, recycle, biogas or save energy::led,trees,recycle,biogas,switch off,save"),
S("Define energy sustainability and explain why it is important for schools",3,"Energy sustainability is using energy in ways that meet present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. It saves schools money, reduces waste and protects the environment.",
  "Using energy to meet present needs without harming the ability of future generations::present needs,future generations,future,generations|It saves money and reduces waste::save,money,waste,cost|It protects the environment::environment,pollution,clean"),
S("What is an energy audit",4,"An energy audit is a systematic survey of how energy is used in a place, identifying waste. In a classroom I would walk through, note every light, fan and appliance, record whether they are left on when not needed, note the type of bulb and calculate the wasted hours.",
  "A systematic survey of how energy is used in a place::survey,systematic,how energy is used|It identifies waste and ways to reduce use::waste,reduce,wasted|Walk through and list lights, fans and appliances::lights,fans,appliances,devices,list|Note which are left on unnecessarily and the type of bulb::on,unnecessarily,bulb,bulbs,left on"),
S("List the seven steps in designing a local energy sustainability project",7,"Identify the problem; research solutions; set clear goals; plan the project; carry it out; monitor and evaluate; report and share.",STEPS),
S("Suggest a suitable energy sustainability project for a school in a rural area",4,"A school solar lighting project: install solar panels to power LED lights in classrooms and the compound. It reduces electricity cost, gives reliable lighting and reduces dependence on the grid; follow the seven steps from survey to report.",
  "Solar panels powering LED lights in classrooms and the compound::solar,panels,led,lights,classrooms|Benefit: reliable lighting or lower cost::reliable,cost,cheaper,save|Benefit: no dependence on the grid::grid,dependence,independent|Follow the steps (survey, goals, plan, monitor, report)::survey,goals,plan,monitor,report,steps"),
S("Explain how replacing incandescent bulbs with LED lights in a school reduces both",3,"LED bulbs use up to 80 percent less electricity for the same brightness, so the electricity bill falls; using less electricity from fossil-fuel power stations means less CO₂ is released.",
  "LEDs use much less electricity (up to 80%) for the same brightness::less electricity,80,less,same brightness|So electricity costs fall::cost,costs,bill,bills,cheaper|Less electricity means less CO₂ released::co2,carbon dioxide,emissions,greenhouse"),
S("Describe how a biogas digester project at a school canteen would work",4,"Food waste and kitchen scraps from the canteen are put in a small digester; bacteria decompose them to produce methane gas, which is used for cooking in place of LPG or firewood. It reduces fuel cost and waste.",
  "Food waste and kitchen scraps are put in the digester::food waste,kitchen,scraps,waste|Bacteria decompose them and produce methane (biogas)::bacteria,decompose,methane,biogas|The gas replaces LPG or firewood for cooking::lpg,firewood,cooking|Benefits: lower fuel cost and less waste::cost,waste,cheaper"),
S("Explain why the 'monitor and evaluate' step is essential",3,"It checks whether the project achieved its goals, shows what worked and what needs improvement. We could compare electricity bills before and after, check that areas are well lit, and survey whether people changed behaviour.",
  "It checks whether the project achieved its goals::goals,achieved,worked|It shows what worked and what needs improvement::improvement,improve,worked|Measure: compare electricity bills before and after::bills,before,after,compare|Or survey changes in behaviour::survey,behaviour"),
S("Describe two ways in which tree planting contributes to energy sustainability",2,"Trees give shade that lowers room temperatures (so less need for fans), and trees absorb CO₂ from the atmosphere, improving the environment.",
  "Shade reduces room temperatures::shade,temperature,temperatures,cool|Trees absorb CO₂::absorb,co2,carbon"),
S("A community currently uses diesel generators for electricity",3,"Diesel generators burn fuel that releases CO₂ and cause pollution, and fuel is expensive to buy. A sustainable alternative is a community solar-powered system or pump, which removes the fuel cost and reduces CO₂ emissions.",
  "Environmental: diesel releases CO₂ and pollution::co2,carbon dioxide,pollution,emissions|Financial: fuel is expensive::fuel,cost,expensive,buy|Alternative: solar power::solar,biogas,wind"),
S("Why is it important to share the results of an energy sustainability project",2,"It encourages other schools and communities to copy the project, so its impact is multiplied.","It encourages others to replicate the project::encourage,replicate,copy,repeat|So the impact is multiplied::multiply,multiplied,impact,more"),
S("Explain the difference between energy sustainability and energy efficiency",3,"Energy sustainability is using energy so that present needs are met without harming future generations' needs, for example using solar power. Energy efficiency is producing the same useful output using less energy, for example an LED bulb instead of an incandescent bulb.",
  "Sustainability: meeting present needs without compromising future generations::present,future,generations|Efficiency: the same useful output using less energy::same,less energy,useful output|An example of each such as solar power and LED bulbs::solar,led,bulb,bulbs"),
S("Design a complete energy sustainability project for your school",6,"For example: (a) lights are left on in empty classrooms; (b) a behaviour campaign and LED bulbs; (c) cut the electricity bill by 20% in three months; (d) bulbs, posters, learners and school management; (e) compare bills and check rooms weekly; (f) present at assembly and share with other schools.",
  "(a) The energy problem observed, such as lights left on::problem,waste,left on,lights|(b) A sustainable solution such as LEDs, solar or a campaign::led,solar,biogas,campaign,solution|(c) Specific goals with numbers and time::goal,goals,reduce,percent,%,months,term|(d) Resources: materials, people and money::resources,materials,people,money,budget|(e) How to monitor, e.g. compare bills::monitor,compare,bills,check|(f) How to report, e.g. assembly or class::report,assembly,present,share"),
S("A district education officer visits your school",3,"Energy sustainability matters because wasted energy costs money. Switching to LED bulbs, switching off lights and using solar can lower bills so the money can be used for learning, and it also reduces CO₂ and protects the environment for future learners.",
  "Saves money by reducing energy waste and bills::save,saves,money,bills,cost,waste|Examples such as LEDs, switching off lights or solar::led,switch off,solar|Protects the environment and the future::environment,co2,future,climate"),
]
